<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Papak</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 210</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century">3rd century</a> · Also known as Pabag, Babak</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Papak, or Pabag, was a local ruler in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persis">Persis</a> in south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> in the early 3rd century CE and father of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ardashir-i">Ardashir I</a>, the founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire</a>. According to tradition he was a priest at the temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anahita">Anahita</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istakhr">Istakhr</a> who seized power in the city around 205, overthrowing the local king. The Sasanian kings honoured him as the founder of their house.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/papak.jpg" alt="Papak" width="320" height="312" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Classical Numismatic Group; [1] · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coin_of_Pabag.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Pabag's homeland, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pars">Pars</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persis">Persis</a> in the south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iranian-plateau">Iranian plateau</a>, had been the birthplace of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a>. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> it was ruled by local dynasties, first under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire</a> and then as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of the Parthian (Arsacid) Empire, whose Kings of Persis used the title of shah. Pabag himself ruled a small principality in the area of Khir, south of Bakhtegan Lake, as a vassal of Gochihr, the Bazrangid king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istakhr">Stakhr</a>, who was in turn a vassal of the Parthian emperor.</p><p>With Gochihr's permission, Pabag sent Ardashir to serve under Tiri, commander of the fortress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darab">Darabgerd</a>. Pabag reportedly served as a priest of the Temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anahita">Anahita</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istakhr">Istakhr</a>, a rallying point for local Persian soldiers who worshipped the goddess. The medieval historian Al-Tabari makes him the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasan">Sasan</a> and of Rambihisht, a princess of the Bazrangid family.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthian-empire">Parthian Empire</a> under Vologases V was weakened by wars with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>, civil wars and regional revolts. The Roman emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/septimius-severus">Septimius Severus</a> invaded in 196 and again two years later, when he sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ctesiphon">Ctesiphon</a>, and revolts broke out in Media and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pars">Pars</a>. In 205 or 206 Pabag rebelled, overthrew Gochihr and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istakhr">Istakhr</a>. Al-Tabari says he acted at Ardashir's urging, but the Iranologist Touraj Daryaee considers this unlikely and holds that it was the eldest son, Shapur, who helped him, as coins bearing the portraits of both show.</p><p>Pabag then named Shapur his heir, much to the dislike of Ardashir, who had become commander of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darab">Darabgerd</a> after the death of Tiri. In an act of defiance Ardashir left for Ardashir-Khwarrah and fortified himself there, preparing to attack his brother Shapur once their father had died.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Pabag died a natural death a few years after taking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istakhr">Istakhr</a> and was succeeded by Shapur. Both brothers then minted coins with the title of king and Pabag's portrait; the reverse of Shapur's read son of His Majesty, King Pabag. Shapur died in obscure circumstances in 211 or 212, and Ardashir succeeded him, conquered the rest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> and established the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire</a> in 224.</p><p>Ardashir's coins and his inscription at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naqsh-e-rostam">Naqsh-e Rostam</a> call him the son of divine Pabag, the king, and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shapur-i">Shapur I</a>, in inscriptions at Naqsh-e Rajab and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaba-ye-zartosht">Ka'ba-ye Zartosht</a>, calls himself the grandson of Pabag. The Middle Persian Kar-Namag i Ardashir i Pabagan, the Book of the Deeds of Ardashir, son of Pabag, keeps the same link in its title.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pabag” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Around 205 CE <b>Pabag</b>, a local lord at Istakhr, seized power, and his son Ardashir made himself king of Persis.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-persis">Kingdom of Persis</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persis">Persis</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=30,52&amp;year=200" aria-label="Persis on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5901 1068 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Papak"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6351 1338h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6351 1338h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=30,52&amp;year=200">What was here in 200 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
